---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BlogPost from '../../components/BlogPost.astro';
import { countries, getReferralUrl } from '../../data/countries';
import { posts } from '../../data/posts';

const country = countries.find((c) => c.slug === 'uk')!;
const referralUrl = getReferralUrl(country);
const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === 'how-tesla-referral-programme-works-uk')!;

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: post.title,
  description: post.description,
  datePublished: post.date,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'UK Tesla Model 3 owner' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Tesla Referral Code UK',
    url: 'https://teslareferralcode.co/',
  },
  mainEntityOfPage: `https://teslareferralcode.co/blog/${post.slug}/`,
};
---
<Layout
  title={`${post.title} | Tesla Referral Code UK`}
  description={post.description}
  country={country}
  canonical={`https://teslareferralcode.co/blog/${post.slug}/`}
  jsonLd={articleJsonLd}
>
  <BlogPost title={post.title} date={post.date} readTime={post.readTime}>
    <p>
      Tesla's UK referral programme is the only official way to get a discount or
      perk on a new Tesla. There are no public discount codes, no seasonal sales
      and no haggling at the showroom. If you want money off, or free miles, you
      need a referral code from an existing owner. Here is how the programme
      actually works in 2026, with the small print most people miss.
    </p>

    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <p>
      The perk depends on which model you order. For new Model 3 and Model Y
      orders, Tesla credits your account with 650 free Supercharging miles. For
      new Model S and Model X orders, Tesla deducts £500 from the purchase
      price at checkout. You can only claim one perk per car, and the perk is
      tied to the order you place, not to the buyer personally.
    </p>

    <h3>Why two different perks?</h3>
    <p>
      Tesla designs the referral incentive around the margin on each model. Model
      3 and Model Y are the higher volume cars and they get a "soft" perk in the
      form of Supercharging credit, which costs Tesla less than handing over cash.
      Model S and Model X are lower volume premium cars and they get a flat £500
      off, which is a more meaningful incentive at that price point.
    </p>

    <h2>Who qualifies</h2>
    <p>
      The referral perk only applies to brand new Tesla orders. It does not work
      on a used Tesla bought through Tesla's own approved-used programme. It also
      does not apply to leases set up by third party finance companies, and it
      does not stack with corporate or salary sacrifice discounts in most cases.
      If you are buying outright, financing through Tesla, or buying personally
      under PCP, you should be fine.
    </p>

    <h2>How to apply the code</h2>
    <p>There are two ways to use a referral code in the UK:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Order online:</strong> click a referral link (like the one on the
        <a href="/">main page</a> of this site) before you start configuring your
        car. The code attaches to your tesla.com session and applies
        automatically when you confirm the order.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Order in store:</strong> tell the advisor you have a referral
        code before they submit the order in their system. They will type it in
        manually. There is a separate <a href="/blog/tesla-referral-code-at-tesla-store/">guide on using a code in store</a>
        if you would rather order in person.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The single most common mistake</h2>
    <p>
      Tesla cannot add a referral code to an order after the order has been
      submitted. There is no support email, no exception process and no way to
      retro-fit the perk. If you confirm an order without the code attached, the
      perk is gone for that purchase. Always check the code is applied before
      you click confirm.
    </p>

    <h2>When the perk shows up</h2>
    <p>
      For the £500 off, it is deducted in your order summary before you pay any
      money. You see it as a line item with the referral code shown next to it.
      For the 650 free Supercharging miles, the credit appears in your Tesla
      account a few days after you take delivery of the car, under the
      "Charging" section in the Tesla app.
    </p>

    <h2>Do the miles expire?</h2>
    <p>
      The free Supercharging miles do not expire in any UK literature Tesla has
      published. People with referrals from years ago still have credit on their
      accounts. That said, Tesla can change the terms of the programme at any
      time, so there is no guarantee they will be valid forever. The sensible
      approach is to use them in your first year of ownership when you are most
      likely to be relying on Superchargers anyway.
    </p>

    <h2>What if I forget to apply the code?</h2>
    <p>
      You cannot retro-apply it. Tesla's official position is that it is the
      buyer's responsibility to make sure the code is on the order at the time of
      purchase. If you are in the showroom right now and have not given a code
      yet, ask the advisor to hold off submitting the order, pull up a referral
      link on your phone, and read out the code before they click submit.
    </p>

    <h2>Ready to use a code?</h2>
    <p>
      If you are about to order a new Tesla in the UK, the referral link on this
      site is ready to use. It opens tesla.com with the code pre-attached.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href={referralUrl} rel="nofollow" class="inline-block mt-2">
        <span class="btn-primary">Use the referral link</span>
      </a>
    </p>

    <h2>Read next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/blog/650-free-supercharging-miles-value/">What can you do with 650 free Supercharging miles?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/blog/tesla-referral-code-at-tesla-store/">How to use a Tesla referral code at a Tesla store</a></li>
      <li><a href="/">Back to the main page and FAQ</a></li>
    </ul>
  </BlogPost>
</Layout>
